\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \lang1033\langfe3082\langnp1033\insrsid9251551  (1) Any person who causes the death of an unborn child shall be liable to deprivation of liberty for a period not exceeding two years.
\par (2) The mother of the unborn child shall not be liable to this penalty.
\par (3) A criminal act as referred to in subsection 1 is not committed by a physician who undertakes this procedure in a health care establishment in the public sector, in cases in which:
\par 1. the pregnancy is endangering the mother's life or seriously jeopardizing her health, according to the diagnosis of two physicians other than the physician carrying out the procedure referred to in subsection 1, such diagnosis being unnece
ssary if the threat to the mother's life has to be eliminated immediately; 
\par 2. the death of the unborn child is due to measures taken to save the mother's life or prevent serious injury to her health, this risk having been diagnosed by two other physicians; 
\par 3. a prenatal diagnosis established by two physicians other than the physician carrying out the procedure referred to in subsection 1 has demonstrated the presence of a serious and irremediable defect in the fetus; or
\par 4. There are valid reasons, confirmed by an attestation on the part of the Office of the Public Prosecutor, for suspecting that the pregnancy resulted from an unlawful act.
\par (4) In certain specific and justified cases, the court may decide not to impose a penalty on the person responsible for a criminal act, as referred to in subsection 1.\'94}}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs28\insrsid9251551 
